#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166550 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166941 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113b981d-bbf2-588a-be74-028506cdb19d" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "8337af7a-73e8-5ae2-9bed-b03f193b2950" :question/math-academy-id "e-21657" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 3]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/2b/2bc77eafe7c4362a1020c1f547a19ef0e72c309ebb82d1480e2ffec5bdfd8b38.svg)\nsketch the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above." :question/worked-solution "We are given that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/b0/b0df9855fa710d30217ffbe12135de27a9aa50e55b639418829b649ee6eb420c.svg)\n\nFor example, when $c_1=1$ and $c_2=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $2.12$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/3c/3c2ea4467854f749e07f5ae3922273a2ebd441da4fb9df834ce0676157e856a2.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(3t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(3t)| \\leq 1.$ Thus, the obtained curve is bounded. So, we have an ellipse that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df8e7ae5f7ff7281cbdbf5849dc94faf59d2421621dc5009b21eeb947485d153.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b542bd66-f4f8-5b4e-a3e7-4717827c427a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"}] :question/id #uuid "7b4f569b-ad3a-5a93-a53e-27a186b7700e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21658" :question/problem "The phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda = a \\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Provide an interpretation of the portrait and determine the possible values of $a.$\n\n![](images/6d/6d13d0dfadac45d40b74cb7e740a880305098f0939e2f45d74706c4022183c54.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is unbounded, representing a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise. Thus, we must have $a \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward the origin.\n\nHence, to insure that $e^{at} \\to 0,$ we require $a < 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166552 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f0b176c-c802-5b3b-85d0-dede602c9bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "d7b40689-7b7e-546e-9b66-30e6a95f3ee0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21659" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda=\\pm2\\text{i}.$ Provide an interpretation of the equilibrium point (origin) for the system of differential equations given above." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a = \\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0$ and $e^{at} = e^{0\\cdot t} = 1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is a center."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f0b176c-c802-5b3b-85d0-dede602c9bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d09c368-867e-5295-888f-2d5522e93320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305264} {:answer/id #uuid "5beb30d1-23c2-5075-8b1d-e091046c0f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306034} {:answer/id #uuid "756d2617-b102-56a0-a522-8dec631437dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d09c368-867e-5295-888f-2d5522e93320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305264} :answer-field/id #uuid "d914bbc6-a082-50f7-9cda-a921caccdc7a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308201} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d876dd6-796c-5def-b786-b647e283b215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305427} {:answer/id #uuid "63a2a224-f9e6-5b06-a51f-fab909a03aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306117} {:answer/id #uuid "9288b3fd-6217-5dc0-a6cc-d0617247a0d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306540} {:answer/id #uuid "a37e88b2-535b-56bf-a159-1c96e37285da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306691} {:answer/id #uuid "b15a28f9-090d-55da-8b02-5c72519ebdaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306817} {:answer/id #uuid "c00d3090-adac-5dc6-9f55-3e991a3b631d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63a2a224-f9e6-5b06-a51f-fab909a03aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306117} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa2c442a-e93e-5d94-8ad6-003d643e840d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09a5051d-6218-52fc-9de8-8eb98b5a1f5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-332952" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =1\\pm 6\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=1>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166941 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113b981d-bbf2-588a-be74-028506cdb19d" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8b0a43-31f1-5fe6-9ec0-7118a4751741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427002} {:answer/id #uuid "89494851-9073-5ac1-be1d-05debbac8065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427039} {:answer/id #uuid "e791d48c-f3d9-5ce4-bf63-a75f6856114a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427087} {:answer/id #uuid "f787aa93-5d84-530b-beae-2c4955937178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e791d48c-f3d9-5ce4-bf63-a75f6856114a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759