427088} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1f1dc9c-4695-5e59-9862-8d072d4ecb63" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99d67f71-7edc-5901-bbd8-911b5a0d051c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333743" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1\\pm 2\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-1}} \\\\ y={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.05$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1.05$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4b/4bcb5580c38573cfdd9839a9d305deec877945a2206aea7db6d69ec37d20ee33.png)\n\nAs $t\\to \\infty ,$ the curve\n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-8}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/07/07d13106e7599d36636da2101ac0bc8fb73b26062416f915a0253097eb85108c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.05$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1.05$ | $2.19$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4b/4bcb5580c38573cfdd9839a9d305deec877945a2206aea7db6d69ec37d20ee33.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)|\\le 1$ and $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)|\\le 1$ but ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, the curve $\\text{diverges}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we have $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nAgain, since ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/07/07d13106e7599d36636da2101ac0bc8fb73b26062416f915a0253097eb85108c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166941 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113b981d-bbf2-588a-be74-028506cdb19d" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2647e169-3dc0-54c7-8b4c-60518680dd89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426996} {:ans